## GitOps Deployment Architecture

The platform uses **Argo CD** as the GitOps continuous delivery controller for Kubernetes workloads and observability components.

The core principle is that **Git is the desired-state source of truth**.

Instead of manually installing platform components with commands such as:

```bash
helm install ...
```

the required Helm chart version, custom values, destination namespace, and synchronisation behaviour are declared through Argo CD `Application` resources.

The deployment flow is:

```text
Developer
    |
    | git commit / push
    v
GitHub GitOps Repository
    |
    | watched by
    v
Argo CD
    |
    | compares desired vs actual state
    v
Amazon EKS
    |
    +-- Application workloads
    |
    +-- Prometheus
    |
    +-- OpenTelemetry Collector
    |
    +-- Loki
    |
    +-- Grafana
```

### Why GitOps?

GitOps provides several operational benefits:

- Kubernetes configuration is version controlled.
- Changes can be reviewed before deployment.
- Git history provides an audit trail.
- Argo CD continuously compares Git with the EKS cluster.
- Configuration drift can be automatically corrected.
- Removed resources can automatically be pruned.
- Platform configuration can be reproduced from the repository.

---

## Argo CD Multi-Source Applications

For third-party observability components, the project uses the **multi-source Application pattern**.

This allows Argo CD to obtain:

1. The official Helm chart from the vendor's Helm repository.
2. Environment-specific `values.yaml` from the project's GitOps repository.

Conceptually:

```text
Official Helm Repository
        |
        | Helm chart
        |
        +-------------------+
                            |
                            v
                         Argo CD
                            ^
                            |
        +-------------------+
        |
        | values.yaml
        |
GitHub GitOps Repository
```

Argo CD combines the official chart with the configuration stored in Git and renders the final Kubernetes manifests.

This avoids copying third-party Helm charts into the project repository while still keeping platform configuration under GitOps control.

---

## Example: Prometheus Argo CD Application

Prometheus was deployed using the official Prometheus Community Helm chart.

The Argo CD Application uses two sources:

```yaml
apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1
kind: Application

metadata:
  name: prometheus
  namespace: argocd

spec:
  project: default

  sources:

    # Official Prometheus Helm Chart
    - repoURL: https://prometheus-community.github.io/helm-charts
      chart: prometheus
      targetRevision: 27.39.0

      helm:
        valueFiles:
          - $values/observability/prometheus/values.yaml

    # GitOps Repository containing our values.yaml
    - repoURL: <gitops-repository>
      targetRevision: main
      ref: values

  destination:
    server: https://kubernetes.default.svc
    namespace: observability

  syncPolicy:
    automated:
      prune: true
      selfHeal: true

    syncOptions:
      - CreateNamespace=true
```

The first source retrieves the official Helm chart.

The second source is assigned:

```yaml
ref: values
```

This allows the first source to reference files from the Git repository using:

```yaml
$values/observability/prometheus/values.yaml
```

Therefore:

```text
$values
   |
   v
GitOps repository
   |
   v
observability/
   |
   v
prometheus/
   |
   v
values.yaml
```

---

## Automated Synchronisation

The applications use:

```yaml
syncPolicy:
  automated:
    prune: true
    selfHeal: true
```

### Self-Heal

```yaml
selfHeal: true
```

If a Kubernetes resource is manually changed and becomes different from the desired configuration stored in Git, Argo CD can restore the Git-defined configuration.

Conceptually:

```text
Git desired state
      |
      | comparison
      v
Argo CD
      |
      +---- Cluster matches Git
      |
      +---- Cluster differs from Git
                    |
                    v
              Self-heal
                    |
                    v
            Restore desired state
```

This is why production configuration changes should normally be made through Git rather than manually modifying Argo CD-managed resources with `kubectl edit`.

### Pruning

```yaml
prune: true
```

When a resource is removed from the desired configuration, Argo CD can remove the corresponding Kubernetes resource from the cluster.

This prevents obsolete resources from remaining in EKS after they have been removed from Git.

---

## Namespace Creation

The applications also use:

```yaml
syncOptions:
  - CreateNamespace=true
```

This allows Argo CD to create the destination namespace when required.

The observability components were deployed into:

```text
observability
```

while Argo CD itself runs in:

```text
argocd
```

---

## GitOps Change Workflow

A typical platform configuration change followed this workflow:

```text
Modify values.yaml
        |
        v
git add
        |
        v
git commit
        |
        v
git push
        |
        v
GitHub
        |
        v
Argo CD detects new revision
        |
        v
Helm chart + values.yaml rendered
        |
        v
Kubernetes manifests applied
        |
        v
EKS workload updated
```

For example, when changing the OpenTelemetry Collector configuration:

```bash
git add observability/otel-collector/values.yaml

git commit -m "Update OpenTelemetry collector configuration"

git push origin main
```

Argo CD then reconciles the desired configuration with the resources running in EKS.

---

## Verifying Argo CD State

The Kubernetes representation of an Argo CD Application can be inspected with:

```bash
kubectl get application <application-name> -n argocd
```

For more detail:

```bash
kubectl describe application <application-name> -n argocd
```

The complete Application specification and status can be inspected with:

```bash
kubectl get application <application-name> \
  -n argocd \
  -o yaml
```

This became particularly useful when troubleshooting Helm chart versions, Git revisions, and multi-source `valueFiles` paths.

---

## Key GitOps Lesson

One of the important lessons from this implementation was that there are multiple layers of configuration:

```text
Git values.yaml
      |
      v
Argo CD Application
      |
      v
Helm rendering
      |
      v
Generated Kubernetes resource
      |
      v
Running Pod
```

When troubleshooting, checking only `values.yaml` is not always sufficient.

The effective configuration should also be verified at the Kubernetes level.

For example:

```bash
kubectl get configmap <configmap-name> -n observability -o yaml
```

or:

```bash
kubectl get statefulset <name> -n observability -o yaml
```

This verifies what configuration Argo CD and Helm actually rendered and deployed rather than assuming that the intended Git configuration reached the workload successfully.